/-!
# Projective embeddings of a finite fibre product and its closed subschemes

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 01WD, 01KR, 0C4Q, 01WC and 01T6
(blueprint: lem-sfb-projective-fibre-product-closed-model).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The canonical comparison from the finite fibre product over `X` to the fibre product
of the same factors over `S`. Its components are the original projections, and its base
map is the original base map followed by `f`.

Source: Stacks Project, Schemes, Tag 01KR; finite iteration of the comparison there
(blueprint: lem-sfb-projective-fibre-product-closed-model). -/
noncomputable def projectiveFibreProductComparison
    {I : Type u} [Fintype I] {X S : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ S)
    (Y : I → Scheme.{u}) (q : ∀ i : I, Y i ⟶ X) :
    widePullback X Y q ⟶ widePullback S Y (fun i ↦ q i ≫ f) :=
  WidePullback.lift (WidePullback.base q ≫ f) (WidePullback.π q)
    (fun i ↦ (Category.assoc (WidePullback.π q i) (q i) f).symm.trans
      (congrArg (fun a ↦ a ≫ f) (WidePullback.π_arrow q i)))

/-- Finite products over a separated scheme of schemes embedded in a fixed finite
projective space over a field admit the specified projective embedding. Every closed
subscheme has absolute and relative embeddings with the same dimension bound, the stated
properness, finite type and Noetherianity, and surjectivity when it is an isomorphism over
a dense open of the target.

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 01WD, 01KR, 0C4Q, 01WC and 01T6
(blueprint: lem-sfb-projective-fibre-product-closed-model).

Proof:
1. Enumerate the nonempty finite index set. Induct on its size to show that the comparison
   to the product over the field is closed. The one-factor comparison is an identity.
   The next comparison factors through the product over the field of the preceding
   factors and the next factor: the first arrow is closed by the separated comparison
   lemma (lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-change-of-base-closed-immersion), and the second is a
   base change of the preceding comparison. Use lem-weil-pbc-closed-immersion-base-change
   and lem-weil-pbc-closed-immersion-comp. Universal properties preserve all projections.
2. Inductively embed the product over the field by the given embeddings and the Segre
   embedding (lem-chow-segre). The dimension recurrence is
   M_(j+1) + 1 = (M_j + 1)(n + 1), giving (n + 1)^|I| - 1. Compose with step 1.
3. Compose the resulting embedding with the given closed immersion c to embed T over
   the field. The graph of q = c followed by the product base map is closed because f
   is separated, by the comparison lemma. Follow this graph by the base change of the
   absolute embedding of T. The projective-space base-change identification
   (lemma-nagata-projective-space-base-change-pullback) gives the required embedding
   into projective space over X, with the same dimension and structural projection q.
4. These factorizations give IsHProjective for P over the field, T over the field, and q.
   Apply lem-kun-projective-implies-proper to all three. Proper morphisms are locally of
   finite type and quasi-compact, hence of finite type in def-weil-finite-type-morphism.
5. A field is Noetherian: a nonzero ideal contains a unit and is the unit ideal, while
   the zero ideal is generated by zero. Its affine spectrum is locally Noetherian and
   compact, having one point (def-weil-field, def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring,
   def-weil-affine-spectrum, pqf-def-locnoeth, def-weil-pbc-quasi-compact-scheme).
6. Apply lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian to P and T over this spectrum. This
   yields both local Noetherianity and compactness, including for empty T.
7. If q restricts to an isomorphism over a dense open V, its image contains V. Properness
   makes its image closed. It therefore contains the closure of V, which is X, giving
   surjectivity (def-weil-proper-morphism, def-weil-surjective-morphism). -/
theorem projectiveFibreProductClosedModel
    (k : Type u) [Field k] {X : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ Spec (.of k)) [IsSeparated f]
    (I : Type u) [Fintype I] [Nonempty I] (n : ℕ)
    (Y : I → Scheme.{u}) (q : ∀ i : I, Y i ⟶ X)
    (e : ∀ i : I, Y i ⟶ ℙ(ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1)); Spec (.of k)))
    (he : ∀ i : I, IsClosedImmersion (e i))
    (he_over : ∀ i : I, e i ≫ ℙ(ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1)); Spec (.of k)) ↘
      Spec (.of k) = q i ≫ f) :
    IsClosedImmersion (projectiveFibreProductComparison f Y q) ∧
    (∃ eP : widePullback X Y q ⟶
        ℙ(ULift.{u} (Fin ((n + 1) ^ Nat.card I - 1 + 1)); Spec (.of k)),
      IsClosedImmersion eP ∧
      eP ≫ ℙ(ULift.{u} (Fin ((n + 1) ^ Nat.card I - 1 + 1)); Spec (.of k)) ↘
        Spec (.of k) = WidePullback.base q ≫ f) ∧
    IsHProjective (WidePullback.base q ≫ f) ∧
    IsProper (WidePullback.base q ≫ f) ∧
    LocallyOfFiniteType (WidePullback.base q ≫ f) ∧
    QuasiCompact (WidePullback.base q ≫ f) ∧
    IsLocallyNoetherian (widePullback X Y q) ∧ CompactSpace (widePullback X Y q : Scheme.{u}) ∧
    (∀ {T : Scheme.{u}} (c : T ⟶ widePullback X Y q), IsClosedImmersion c →
      (∃ eT : T ⟶
          ℙ(ULift.{u} (Fin ((n + 1) ^ Nat.card I - 1 + 1)); Spec (.of k)),
        IsClosedImmersion eT ∧
        eT ≫ ℙ(ULift.{u} (Fin ((n + 1) ^ Nat.card I - 1 + 1)); Spec (.of k)) ↘
          Spec (.of k) = (c ≫ WidePullback.base q) ≫ f) ∧
      (∃ dT : T ⟶ ℙ(ULift.{u} (Fin ((n + 1) ^ Nat.card I - 1 + 1)); X),
        IsClosedImmersion dT ∧
        dT ≫ ℙ(ULift.{u} (Fin ((n + 1) ^ Nat.card I - 1 + 1)); X) ↘ X =
          c ≫ WidePullback.base q) ∧
      IsHProjective ((c ≫ WidePullback.base q) ≫ f) ∧
      IsHProjective (c ≫ WidePullback.base q) ∧
      IsProper ((c ≫ WidePullback.base q) ≫ f) ∧
      IsProper (c ≫ WidePullback.base q) ∧
      LocallyOfFiniteType ((c ≫ WidePullback.base q) ≫ f) ∧
      QuasiCompact ((c ≫ WidePullback.base q) ≫ f) ∧
      LocallyOfFiniteType (c ≫ WidePullback.base q) ∧
      QuasiCompact (c ≫ WidePullback.base q) ∧
      IsLocallyNoetherian T ∧ CompactSpace T ∧
      ((∃ V : X.Opens, Dense (V : Set X) ∧
        IsIso (pullback.snd (c ≫ WidePullback.base q) V.ι)) →
        Surjective (c ≫ WidePullback.base q))) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
